  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 345 PM EDT Wednesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...The threat of severe thunderstorms will \ncontinue this afternoon and the potential for torrential \nrainfall to produce scattered to numerous instances of flash \nflooding will increase tonight into Thursday.\n\nGOES water vapor channels depict a vigorous upper trough from \nthe Great Lakes to the Northeast with the Mid-Atlantic at the \nbase of the upper trough. Locally, there is a moist frontal band\nalong the base of the trough producing persistent showers/tstms\nfrom southside to east central VA. The cold front is lagging to\nthe NW, and scattered showers/tstms have developed from central\nVA to the MD Eastern Shore where there has been some better \nsurface heating. The environment remains rather favorable for a \nfew severe tstms with 40- 50kt of effective bulk shear and \n~1500J/kg of MLCAPE. The main threat is locally damaging wind \ngusts, although a brief tornado/waterspout is possible near the \ncoastlines. Additionally, locally heavy rain remains possible \nwith PW values of 2.0-2.3\".\n\nAttention then turns to the heavy rain/significant flash \nflooding threat. Despite the ongoing drought conditions, there \nremains a confident signal for flash flooding, especially in \nurban and poor drainage areas. WPC has continued with a Moderate\nRisk ERO for tonight across much of Hampton Roads down into \nnorthern portions of NC. Much of the remainder of the area is in\na Slight Risk ERO today. Decent flow aloft should keep \nindividual cells moving but training storms on top of the \nrainfall received yesterday and overnight early this morning \nwill likely cause scattered to numerous instances of flash \nflooding. The axis of heavier showers and storms shifts further \nto the SE on Thursday as the slow moving front gradually sinks \nsouth. WPC has continued a Day 2 Moderate ERO across northeast \nNC up to roughly the VA border and a Slight ERO across southeast\nVA. A Flood Watch continues for SE VA and NE NC through \nThursday, and the Flood Watch from south central VA across the \nPeninsula to the VA Eastern Shore has been extended through noon\nThursday. The Flood Watch from the RIC metro NW and N remains \nin effect through midnight as the heaviest rain should trend \nS/SE of this area later tonight. Additionally, the Flood Watch \nfor portions of the Lower MD Eastern Shore has been extended \nthrough midnight to account for some scattered lingering \nconvection that is capable of producing brief heavy rainfall \nacross areas the received 2-4\" last night.\n\nAll total, the QPF forecast through Thursday has generally 3-5\", \nwith locally higher amounts across southside Hampton Roads into \nnortheast North Carolina.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Below average temperatures are expected Friday \nthrough the weekend, before trending back to seasonal for late \nJuly early next week. There is a general trend toward drier \nconditions through the weekend into early next week.\n\nThe trend is for the cold front to push south of the area later\nThursday into Friday, but there is enough lingering moisture \nfor at least some chc to low-end likely PoPs in far SE VA and NE\nNC. Rain chances then diminish to near to below climo for the \nweekend into early next week. Below average temperatures with \nhighs in the lower to mid 80s are expected Friday through the \nweekend, with dewpoints even dropping into the lower to mid 60s \nacross the northern tier of the area. Temperatures return to \nnear seasonal averages early next week with increasing humidity.",
